Exit like the initial tracee, and end the rest gracefully

PRoot exited with the status of whichever tracee exited last, and a
tracee killed by a signal never set it: a shell's `exit 0` could be
reported as a child's failure, and a shell killed by SIGKILL as some
earlier command's code. PRoot now reports the initial tracee's own exit
status, and when that tracee was killed by a signal PRoot dies by the
same signal, so waitpid(2) on the host sees the truth.

Ending a session is now graceful. With --kill-on-exit the remaining
tracees get SIGHUP -- what a closing terminal delivers -- instead of
SIGKILL at once. A SIGHUP or SIGTERM sent to PRoot itself, which PRoot
used to ignore, is passed on to every tracee the same way; that is how
closing a terminal tab, or asking a git command to stop, reaches the
programs inside. In both cases tracees still alive two seconds later
are killed. The request is checked right before waitpid(2), and its
handler also arms a short wake-up timer, so an idle guest cannot keep
it waiting; that timer is disarmed as soon as waitpid(2) returns, like
the shadow-pipe timer.

PTRACE_O_EXITKILL is set, so no tracee outlives PRoot. Killing PRoot
used to leave its tracees running untraced, outside the guest's view of
the file system (measured: TracerPid 0, reparented to init).

